{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_bdrk_014coho6cW86CRuL8bXga2Ut","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→\\documentclass[11pt]{amsart}\n     2→\\usepackage{amsmath,amsthm,amssymb,bm}\n     3→\\usepackage{xcolor}\n     4→\\usepackage{indentfirst}\n     5→\\usepackage{graphicx}\n     6→\\usepackage{tikz}\n     7→\\usetikzlibrary{decorations.pathreplacing, decorations.pathmorphing, decorations.shapes}\n     8→\\usetikzlibrary{arrows,calc}\n     9→\\usepackage{mathrsfs,esint}\n    10→\\usepackage{comment}\n    11→\\usepackage{enumerate}\n    12→\\usepackage{hyperref}\n    13→\\hypersetup{\n    14→    colorlinks = true,\n    15→    linkcolor = red,\n    16→    anchorcolor = green!50!black,\n    17→    citecolor = green!50!black,\n    18→    filecolor = green!50!black,\n    19→    urlcolor = green!50!black\n    20→}\n    21→\\usepackage{cleveref}\n    22→\n    23→\\usepackage[top=1in, bottom=1in, left=1.25in, right=1.25in]{geometry}\n    24→\\usepackage{setspace}\n    25→\n    26→\\usepackage{float}\n    27→\n    28→\\usepackage{subcaption}\n    29→\\usepackage{pgfplots}\n    30→\\pgfplotsset{compat=newest, compat/show suggested version=false}\n    31→\\usepackage{tikz}\n    32→\\usetikzlibrary{intersections}\n    33→\n    34→\\usepackage{diagbox}\n    35→\\usepackage{algorithm, algorithmicx, algpseudocode}\n    36→\\numberwithin{algorithm}{section}\n    37→\\usepackage{tabularx}\n    38→\\usepackage{tcolorbox}\n    39→\n    40→\\usepackage[titletoc,toc,title]{appendix}\n    41→\\renewcommand\\appendixname{Appendix}\n    42→\n    43→\\newtheorem{thm}{Theorem}[section]\n    44→\\newtheorem{theorem}[thm]{Theorem}\n    45→\\newtheorem{proposition}[thm]{Proposition}\n    46→\\newtheorem{corollary}[thm]{Corollary}\n    47→\\newtheorem{lemma}[thm]{Lemma}\n    48→\\newtheorem{remark}[thm]{\\textit{Remark}}\n    49→\\newtheorem{definition}[thm]{Definition}\n    50→\\newtheorem{assumption}[thm]{Assumption}\n    51→\\newtheorem{example}[thm]{Example}\n    52→\n    53→\\newcommand{\\titleShort}{Wave PINNs}\n    54→\\newcommand{\\titleLong}{Precise Goal and Verification Conditions for the Weight Construction}\n    55→\n    56→\\title[\\titleShort]{\\titleLong}\n    57→\n    58→\\thanks{}\n    59→\n    60→\\author{Author}\n    61→% \\address{Address}\n    62→% \\email{Email}\n    63→\n    64→\\date{}\n    65→\n    66→% \\renewcommand \\theequation {\\arabic{section}.\\arabic{equation}}\n    67→\\numberwithin{equation}{section}\n    68→\n    69→\\begin{document}\n    70→% \\maketitle\n    71→\n    72→%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%\n    73→\\section{Precise Goal}\n    74→\\begin{tcolorbox}[colback=green!5, colframe=green!75!blue, title=Goal with necessary conditions]\n    75→For given arbitrary wave speed $c > 0$ and terminal time $T > 0$, find\n    76→\\begin{equation*}\n    77→    \\left\\{\n    78→    \\begin{aligned}\n    79→        &\\psi(x, t) && \\text{function of $x$ and $t$},\\\\\n    80→        &s          && \\text{negative constant},\\\\\n    81→        &\\alpha     && \\text{constant},\\\\\n    82→        &\\lambda    && \\text{constant},\n    83→    \\end{aligned}\n    84→    \\right.\n    85→\\end{equation*}\n    86→such that the following necessary conditions are satisfied for all $(x, t) \\in [0, \\infty) \\times [0, T]$\n    87→\\begin{equation*}\n    88→    \\left\\{\n    89→    \\begin{aligned}\n    90→        &L_{1} \\psi < L \\psi < - L_{1} \\psi,\\\\\n    91→        &\\lim_{x \\to + \\infty} \\lambda \\psi = + \\infty,\\\\\n    92→        &L_{2} \\psi > 0.\n    93→    \\end{aligned}\n    94→    \\right.\n    95→\\end{equation*}\n    96→\\end{tcolorbox}\n    97→\n    98→\\begin{tcolorbox}[colback=orange!5, colframe=orange!80!blue, title=Sufficient condition]\n    99→The final verification concerns the sufficient condition for all $(x, t) \\in [0, \\infty) \\times [0, T]$\n   100→\\begin{equation*}\n   101→    (L_{1} \\psi)^{2} - (L \\psi)^{2}\n   102→    \\geq\n   103→    c^{2} (\\partial_{x, t}^{2} \\varphi)^{2}.\n   104→\\end{equation*}\n   105→\\end{tcolorbox}\n   106→\n   107→\\begin{tcolorbox}[colback=blue!5, colframe=blue!70, title=Definitions and notations]\n   108→\\begin{enumerate}\n   109→    \\item $\\varphi$: function of $x$ and $t$,\n   110→    \\begin{equation*}\n   111→        \\varphi\n   112→        =\n   113→        \\exp(\\lambda \\psi).\n   114→    \\end{equation*}\n   115→\n   116→    \\item $\\square$: operator acting on functions of $x$ and $t$,\n   117→    \\begin{equation*}\n   118→        \\square u\n   119→        =\n   120→        \\partial_{t}^{2} u - c^{2} \\partial_{x}^{2} u.\n   121→    \\end{equation*}\n   122→\n   123→    \\item $\\widetilde{\\square}$: operator acting on functions of $x$ and $t$,\n   124→    \\begin{equation*}\n   125→        \\widetilde{\\square} u\n   126→        =\n   127→        |\\partial_{t} u|^{2} - c^{2}|\\partial_{x} u|^{2}.\n   128→    \\end{equation*}\n   129→\n   130→    \\item $L$: operator acting on $\\psi$,\n   131→    \\begin{equation*}\n   132→        L \\psi\n   133→        =\n   134→        \\lambda^{2} \\varphi (\\widetilde{\\square} \\psi)\n   135→        -\n   136→        (\\alpha - 1) \\lambda \\varphi (\\square \\psi).\n   137→    \\end{equation*}\n   138→\n   139→    \\item $L_{1}$: operator acting on $\\psi$,\n   140→    \\begin{equation*}\n   141→        \\begin{aligned}\n   142→            L_{1} \\psi\n   143→            &=\n   144→            c^{2} \\partial_{x}^{2} \\varphi\n   145→            +\n   146→            \\partial_{t}^{2} \\varphi\\\\\n   147→            &=\n   148→            \\lambda^{2} \\varphi\n   149→            \\left (\n   150→                (\\partial_{t} \\psi)^{2}\n   151→                +\n   152→                c^{2} (\\partial_{x} \\psi)^{2}\n   153→            \\right )\n   154→            +\n   155→            \\lambda \\varphi\n   156→            \\left (\n   157→                \\partial_{t}^{2} \\psi\n   158→                +\n   159→                c^{2} \\partial_{x}^{2} \\psi\n   160→            \\right ).\n   161→        \\end{aligned}\n   162→    \\end{equation*}\n   163→\n   164→    \\item $L_{2}$: operator acting on $\\psi$,\n   165→    \\begin{equation*}\n   166→    \\begin{aligned}\n   167→        L_{2}\n   168→        &=\n   169→        \\frac{1}{2}\n   170→        \\Big (\n   171→            (\\alpha - 1) s \\lambda (\\square (\\varphi (\\square \\psi)))\n   172→            -\n   173→            s \\lambda^{2} (\\square (\\varphi (\\widetilde{\\square} \\psi)))\n   174→        \\Big )\\\\\n   175→        &+\n   176→        \\Big (\n   177→            s^{3} \\lambda^{3} (\\alpha - 1) \\varphi^{3} (\\widetilde{\\square} \\psi) (\\square \\psi)\n   178→            -\n   179→            s^{3} \\lambda^{4} \\varphi^{3} (\\widetilde{\\square} \\psi)^{2}\\\\\n   180→            &\\qquad+\n   181→            s^{3} \\lambda^{3}\n   182→            \\left (\n   183→                \\partial_{t} (\\varphi^{3} (\\widetilde{\\square} \\psi) (\\partial_{t} \\psi))\n   184→                +\n   185→                c^{2} \\partial_{x}(\\varphi^{3} (\\widetilde{\\square} \\psi) (\\partial_{x} \\psi))\n   186→            \\right )\n   187→        \\Big ).\n   188→    \\end{aligned}\n   189→    \\end{equation*}\n   190→\\end{enumerate}\n   191→\\end{tcolorbox}\n   192→Note that $\\partial_{x}^{2} u = \\partial_{x} (\\partial_{x} u) = u_{x x}$.\n   193→This problem comes from the Carleman estimate for the wave operator $\\square$.\n   194→$\\psi, s, \\alpha, \\lambda$ could be dependent on $c$ and $T$.\n   195→If multiple $\\psi$ are possible, find the smoothest one.\n   196→\n   197→\\end{document}\n   198→\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b0a4aefa-94cf-4571-b6fe-4e02cd213274","uuid":"4923e1c8-3d00-4cb9-8ced-6a33bbebcd92","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/local/home/cyanz/wave_PINN/problem.tex","content":"\\documentclass[11pt]{amsart}\n\\usepackage{amsmath,amsthm,amssymb,bm}\n\\usepackage{xcolor}\n\\usepackage{indentfirst}\n\\usepackage{graphicx}\n\\usepackage{tikz}\n\\usetikzlibrary{decorations.pathreplacing, decorations.pathmorphing, decorations.shapes}\n\\usetikzlibrary{arrows,calc}\n\\usepackage{mathrsfs,esint}\n\\usepackage{comment}\n\\usepackage{enumerate}\n\\usepackage{hyperref}\n\\hypersetup{\n    colorlinks = true,\n    linkcolor = red,\n    anchorcolor = green!50!black,\n    citecolor = green!50!black,\n    filecolor = green!50!black,\n    urlcolor = green!50!black\n}\n\\usepackage{cleveref}\n\n\\usepackage[top=1in, bottom=1in, left=1.25in, right=1.25in]{geometry}\n\\usepackage{setspace}\n\n\\usepackage{float}\n\n\\usepackage{subcaption}\n\\usepackage{pgfplots}\n\\pgfplotsset{compat=newest, compat/show suggested version=false}\n\\usepackage{tikz}\n\\usetikzlibrary{intersections}\n\n\\usepackage{diagbox}\n\\usepackage{algorithm, algorithmicx, algpseudocode}\n\\numberwithin{algorithm}{section}\n\\usepackage{tabularx}\n\\usepackage{tcolorbox}\n\n\\usepackage[titletoc,toc,title]{appendix}\n\\renewcommand\\appendixname{Appendix}\n\n\\newtheorem{thm}{Theorem}[section]\n\\newtheorem{theorem}[thm]{Theorem}\n\\newtheorem{proposition}[thm]{Proposition}\n\\newtheorem{corollary}[thm]{Corollary}\n\\newtheorem{lemma}[thm]{Lemma}\n\\newtheorem{remark}[thm]{\\textit{Remark}}\n\\newtheorem{definition}[thm]{Definition}\n\\newtheorem{assumption}[thm]{Assumption}\n\\newtheorem{example}[thm]{Example}\n\n\\newcommand{\\titleShort}{Wave PINNs}\n\\newcommand{\\titleLong}{Precise Goal and Verification Conditions for the Weight Construction}\n\n\\title[\\titleShort]{\\titleLong}\n\n\\thanks{}\n\n\\author{Author}\n% \\address{Address}\n% \\email{Email}\n\n\\date{}\n\n% \\renewcommand \\theequation {\\arabic{section}.\\arabic{equation}}\n\\numberwithin{equation}{section}\n\n\\begin{document}\n% \\maketitle\n\n%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%\n\\section{Precise Goal}\n\\begin{tcolorbox}[colback=green!5, colframe=green!75!blue, title=Goal with necessary conditions]\nFor given arbitrary wave speed $c > 0$ and terminal time $T > 0$, find\n\\begin{equation*}\n    \\left\\{\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        &\\psi(x, t) && \\text{function of $x$ and $t$},\\\\\n        &s          && \\text{negative constant},\\\\\n        &\\alpha     && \\text{constant},\\\\\n        &\\lambda    && \\text{constant},\n    \\end{aligned}\n    \\right.\n\\end{equation*}\nsuch that the following necessary conditions are satisfied for all $(x, t) \\in [0, \\infty) \\times [0, T]$\n\\begin{equation*}\n    \\left\\{\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        &L_{1} \\psi < L \\psi < - L_{1} \\psi,\\\\\n        &\\lim_{x \\to + \\infty} \\lambda \\psi = + \\infty,\\\\\n        &L_{2} \\psi > 0.\n    \\end{aligned}\n    \\right.\n\\end{equation*}\n\\end{tcolorbox}\n\n\\begin{tcolorbox}[colback=orange!5, colframe=orange!80!blue, title=Sufficient condition]\nThe final verification concerns the sufficient condition for all $(x, t) \\in [0, \\infty) \\times [0, T]$\n\\begin{equation*}\n    (L_{1} \\psi)^{2} - (L \\psi)^{2}\n    \\geq\n    c^{2} (\\partial_{x, t}^{2} \\varphi)^{2}.\n\\end{equation*}\n\\end{tcolorbox}\n\n\\begin{tcolorbox}[colback=blue!5, colframe=blue!70, title=Definitions and notations]\n\\begin{enumerate}\n    \\item $\\varphi$: function of $x$ and $t$,\n    \\begin{equation*}\n        \\varphi\n        =\n        \\exp(\\lambda \\psi).\n    \\end{equation*}\n\n    \\item $\\square$: operator acting on functions of $x$ and $t$,\n    \\begin{equation*}\n        \\square u\n        =\n        \\partial_{t}^{2} u - c^{2} \\partial_{x}^{2} u.\n    \\end{equation*}\n\n    \\item $\\widetilde{\\square}$: operator acting on functions of $x$ and $t$,\n    \\begin{equation*}\n        \\widetilde{\\square} u\n        =\n        |\\partial_{t} u|^{2} - c^{2}|\\partial_{x} u|^{2}.\n    \\end{equation*}\n\n    \\item $L$: operator acting on $\\psi$,\n    \\begin{equation*}\n        L \\psi\n        =\n        \\lambda^{2} \\varphi (\\widetilde{\\square} \\psi)\n        -\n        (\\alpha - 1) \\lambda \\varphi (\\square \\psi).\n    \\end{equation*}\n\n    \\item $L_{1}$: operator acting on $\\psi$,\n    \\begin{equation*}\n        \\begin{aligned}\n            L_{1} \\psi\n            &=\n            c^{2} \\partial_{x}^{2} \\varphi\n            +\n            \\partial_{t}^{2} \\varphi\\\\\n            &=\n            \\lambda^{2} \\varphi\n            \\left (\n                (\\partial_{t} \\psi)^{2}\n                +\n                c^{2} (\\partial_{x} \\psi)^{2}\n            \\right )\n            +\n            \\lambda \\varphi\n            \\left (\n                \\partial_{t}^{2} \\psi\n                +\n                c^{2} \\partial_{x}^{2} \\psi\n            \\right ).\n        \\end{aligned}\n    \\end{equation*}\n\n    \\item $L_{2}$: operator acting on $\\psi$,\n    \\begin{equation*}\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        L_{2}\n        &=\n        \\frac{1}{2}\n        \\Big (\n            (\\alpha - 1) s \\lambda (\\square (\\varphi (\\square \\psi)))\n            -\n            s \\lambda^{2} (\\square (\\varphi (\\widetilde{\\square} \\psi)))\n        \\Big )\\\\\n        &+\n        \\Big (\n            s^{3} \\lambda^{3} (\\alpha - 1) \\varphi^{3} (\\widetilde{\\square} \\psi) (\\square \\psi)\n            -\n            s^{3} \\lambda^{4} \\varphi^{3} (\\widetilde{\\square} \\psi)^{2}\\\\\n            &\\qquad+\n            s^{3} \\lambda^{3}\n            \\left (\n                \\partial_{t} (\\varphi^{3} (\\widetilde{\\square} \\psi) (\\partial_{t} \\psi))\n                +\n                c^{2} \\partial_{x}(\\varphi^{3} (\\widetilde{\\square} \\psi) (\\partial_{x} \\psi))\n            \\right )\n        \\Big ).\n    \\end{aligned}\n    \\end{equation*}\n\\end{enumerate}\n\\end{tcolorbox}\nNote that $\\partial_{x}^{2} u = \\partial_{x} (\\partial_{x} u) = u_{x x}$.\nThis problem comes from the Carleman estimate for the wave operator $\\square$.\n$\\psi, s, \\alpha, \\lambda$ could be dependent on $c$ and $T$.\nIf multiple $\\psi$ are possible, find the smoothest one.\n\n\\end{document}\n","numLines":198,"startLine":1,"totalLines":198}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_bdrk_01EbgSrtQ7qdg6nWjRryCGYb","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→# Failed Approaches Log\n     2→\n     3→This file accumulates all previously tried candidates and why they failed.\n     4→The search agent should read this to avoid repeating dead ends.\n     5→\n     6→---\n     7→\n     8→\n     9→## Round 1\n    10→\n    11→### Candidate\n    12→```python\n    13→# Description: Log-sqrt ansatz: psi = -ln(1+t) - sqrt(x+1), small lambda\n    14→#\n    15→# Key properties:\n    16→# - psi_t = -1/(1+t) -> 0 (bounded derivatives in t)\n    17→# - psi_tt = 1/(1+t)^2 > 0 (convex in t)\n    18→# - psi_x = -1/(2*sqrt(x+1)) -> 0 (derivatives decay in x)\n    19→# - psi_xx = 1/(4*(x+1)^(3/2)) (concave-up correction)\n    20→#\n    21→# With alpha=1:\n    22→# - Condition 1b is EXACTLY: (-L1-L)/phi = c^2/(8*(x+1)^(3/2)) > 0 always\n    23→# - Condition 1a: (L-L1)/phi > 0 verified numerically (holds for t << 1/|lam|^(3/2))\n    24→# - Condition 2: lam*psi = |lam|*(ln(1+t)+sqrt(x+1)) -> +inf as x->inf\n    25→\n    26→psi = -sp.ln(1 + t) - sp.sqrt(x + 1)\n    27→\n    28→subs_dict = {\n    29→    alpha : 1,\n    30→    s     : -1,\n    31→    lam   : sp.Rational(-1, 10),\n    32→}\n    33→```\n    34→\n    35→### Why it failed\n    36→## Failure Analysis\n    37→\n    38→### Root Cause: Unbounded growth of (1+t)² terms relative to spatial terms\n    39→\n    40→All three failing conditions share the same structural weakness: the candidate ψ = -log(1+t) - √(x+1) produces operators where certain terms grow as powers of (1+t)² or (1+t)⁴ in the numerator, while the compensating positive spatial terms (like (x+1)^(3/2)) are independent of t. For any fixed x > 24, increasing t eventually overwhelms the spatial terms.\n    41→\n    42→**Specifically:**\n    43→1. **Condition 1a** numerator = c²(1+t)²(5 - √(x+1)) + 20(x+1)^(3/2). The first term is O(t²) and negative for x > 24; the second term is O(1) in t. So the condition fails for t > O(1/c).\n    44→\n    45→2. **Condition 1b** works because both terms in its numerator are positive — this is the key asymmetry. The (1+t)² growth in condition 1b is in a *positive* coefficient (5c²(1+t)²), whereas in condition 1a it multiplies a coefficient that changes sign.\n    46→\n    47→3. **Conditions 3 and sufficient** inherit the same problem through the operators L and L₁ whose difference involves condition-1a-type expressions raised to higher powers.\n    48→\n    49→### Why the engine reported \"unknown\" instead of \"False\"\n    50→\n    51→The verify engine:\n    52→- Uses SymPy's symbolic sign-checking, which returned \"unknown\" because x, t are declared as `real=True` (not `nonnegative=True`), and the expressions involve √(x+1) which SymPy cannot reason about for arbitrary real x.\n    53→- Falls back to numerical sampling on x ∈ [0, 50], t ∈ [0, 10], c ∈ {0.5, 1, 2}. This range is too small to detect failures that occur at t > 28 (for c=2) or t > 57 (for c=1).\n    54→\n    55→### What the next candidate needs\n    56→\n    57→To fix condition 1a, the candidate must ensure that the expression `(L - L₁)/φ` does not have terms that grow with t while being negative for large x. Two strategies:\n    58→\n    59→1. **Make ψ_x decay faster.** The problematic term is `-2c²λ²(ψ_x)²` in `(L-L₁)/φ`. With ψ_x = -1/(2√(x+1)), we get (ψ_x)² = 1/(4(x+1)), which decays only as O(1/x). If ψ_x decayed faster (e.g., exponentially), the negative contribution would be negligible.\n    60→\n    61→2. **Make the \"Laplacian\" ψ_tt + c²ψ_xx grow with t.** Currently ψ_tt = 1/(1+t)² which *decays* with t. If instead ψ_tt grew or stayed constant, it could compensate the growing negative terms. However, ψ_tt > 0 requires concavity which is hard to maintain with growing second derivatives.\n    62→\n    63→3. **Use a separable form ψ(x,t) = f(x) + g(t) where both f and g are chosen so that the cross-terms in the operators maintain consistent signs.** The current failure arises because the x-dependent sign change at x=24 (where √(x+1) = 5 = 1/(2|λ|)) interacts with t-growth. Choosing |λ| even smaller would push this threshold to larger x, but would not eliminate the asymptotic failure.\n    64→\n    65→4. **Consider ψ forms where ψ_x is bounded by a function of t that decays appropriately.** For instance, ψ = -log(1+t) - log(1+x) would give ψ_x = -1/(1+x) with (ψ_x)² = 1/(1+x)² decaying as O(1/x²), and ψ_xx = 1/(1+x)² > 0. But one must check that condition 2 (λψ → +∞) still holds.\n    66→\n    67→5. **Most promising direction:** The fundamental issue is that (ψ_x)² doesn't decay fast enough relative to ψ_xx. For ψ_x ~ -x^(-p), we have (ψ_x)² ~ x^(-2p) and ψ_xx ~ x^(-p-1). The ratio (ψ_x)²/ψ_xx ~ x^(-p+1). For this ratio to → 0 as x → ∞ (so that the λ² gradient term doesn't dominate the λ Laplacian term), we need p > 1. The current candidate has p = 1/2 (from √(x+1)), so p < 1, which is insufficient. **Try p > 1**, e.g., ψ involving -(x+1)^q for q < 1/2, or -log(x+1), which gives p = 1 (borderline).\n    68→\n    69→---\n    70→\n    71→\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b0a4aefa-94cf-4571-b6fe-4e02cd213274","uuid":"13e39605-de59-4582-9183-545bb1645f2d","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/local/home/cyanz/wave_PINN/candidates/failed_approaches.md","content":"# Failed Approaches Log\n\nThis file accumulates all previously tried candidates and why they failed.\nThe search agent should read this to avoid repeating dead ends.\n\n---\n\n\n## Round 1\n\n### Candidate\n```python\n# Description: Log-sqrt ansatz: psi = -ln(1+t) - sqrt(x+1), small lambda\n#\n# Key properties:\n# - psi_t = -1/(1+t) -> 0 (bounded derivatives in t)\n# - psi_tt = 1/(1+t)^2 > 0 (convex in t)\n# - psi_x = -1/(2*sqrt(x+1)) -> 0 (derivatives decay in x)\n# - psi_xx = 1/(4*(x+1)^(3/2)) (concave-up correction)\n#\n# With alpha=1:\n# - Condition 1b is EXACTLY: (-L1-L)/phi = c^2/(8*(x+1)^(3/2)) > 0 always\n# - Condition 1a: (L-L1)/phi > 0 verified numerically (holds for t << 1/|lam|^(3/2))\n# - Condition 2: lam*psi = |lam|*(ln(1+t)+sqrt(x+1)) -> +inf as x->inf\n\npsi = -sp.ln(1 + t) - sp.sqrt(x + 1)\n\nsubs_dict = {\n    alpha : 1,\n    s     : -1,\n    lam   : sp.Rational(-1, 10),\n}\n```\n\n### Why it failed\n## Failure Analysis\n\n### Root Cause: Unbounded growth of (1+t)² terms relative to spatial terms\n\nAll three failing conditions share the same structural weakness: the candidate ψ = -log(1+t) - √(x+1) produces operators where certain terms grow as powers of (1+t)² or (1+t)⁴ in the numerator, while the compensating positive spatial terms (like (x+1)^(3/2)) are independent of t. For any fixed x > 24, increasing t eventually overwhelms the spatial terms.\n\n**Specifically:**\n1. **Condition 1a** numerator = c²(1+t)²(5 - √(x+1)) + 20(x+1)^(3/2). The first term is O(t²) and negative for x > 24; the second term is O(1) in t. So the condition fails for t > O(1/c).\n\n2. **Condition 1b** works because both terms in its numerator are positive — this is the key asymmetry. The (1+t)² growth in condition 1b is in a *positive* coefficient (5c²(1+t)²), whereas in condition 1a it multiplies a coefficient that changes sign.\n\n3. **Conditions 3 and sufficient** inherit the same problem through the operators L and L₁ whose difference involves condition-1a-type expressions raised to higher powers.\n\n### Why the engine reported \"unknown\" instead of \"False\"\n\nThe verify engine:\n- Uses SymPy's symbolic sign-checking, which returned \"unknown\" because x, t are declared as `real=True` (not `nonnegative=True`), and the expressions involve √(x+1) which SymPy cannot reason about for arbitrary real x.\n- Falls back to numerical sampling on x ∈ [0, 50], t ∈ [0, 10], c ∈ {0.5, 1, 2}. This range is too small to detect failures that occur at t > 28 (for c=2) or t > 57 (for c=1).\n\n### What the next candidate needs\n\nTo fix condition 1a, the candidate must ensure that the expression `(L - L₁)/φ` does not have terms that grow with t while being negative for large x. Two strategies:\n\n1. **Make ψ_x decay faster.** The problematic term is `-2c²λ²(ψ_x)²` in `(L-L₁)/φ`. With ψ_x = -1/(2√(x+1)), we get (ψ_x)² = 1/(4(x+1)), which decays only as O(1/x). If ψ_x decayed faster (e.g., exponentially), the negative contribution would be negligible.\n\n2. **Make the \"Laplacian\" ψ_tt + c²ψ_xx grow with t.** Currently ψ_tt = 1/(1+t)² which *decays* with t. If instead ψ_tt grew or stayed constant, it could compensate the growing negative terms. However, ψ_tt > 0 requires concavity which is hard to maintain with growing second derivatives.\n\n3. **Use a separable form ψ(x,t) = f(x) + g(t) where both f and g are chosen so that the cross-terms in the operators maintain consistent signs.** The current failure arises because the x-dependent sign change at x=24 (where √(x+1) = 5 = 1/(2|λ|)) interacts with t-growth. Choosing |λ| even smaller would push this threshold to larger x, but would not eliminate the asymptotic failure.\n\n4. **Consider ψ forms where ψ_x is bounded by a function of t that decays appropriately.** For instance, ψ = -log(1+t) - log(1+x) would give ψ_x = -1/(1+x) with (ψ_x)² = 1/(1+x)² decaying as O(1/x²), and ψ_xx = 1/(1+x)² > 0. But one must check that condition 2 (λψ → +∞) still holds.\n\n5. **Most promising direction:** The fundamental issue is that (ψ_x)² doesn't decay fast enough relative to ψ_xx. For ψ_x ~ -x^(-p), we have (ψ_x)² ~ x^(-2p) and ψ_xx ~ x^(-p-1). The ratio (ψ_x)²/ψ_xx ~ x^(-p+1). For this ratio to → 0 as x → ∞ (so that the λ² gradient term doesn't dominate the λ Laplacian term), we need p > 1. The current candidate has p = 1/2 (from √(x+1)), so p < 1, which is insufficient. **Try p > 1**, e.g., ψ involving -(x+1)^q for q < 1/2, or -log(x+1), which gives p = 1 (borderline).\n\n---\n\n","numLines":71,"startLine":1,"totalLines":71}}}
